# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# opennext / cloudflare
# cloudflare-env.d.ts is generated by `npm run cf:typegen` but IS committed —
# without it a fresh clone cannot typecheck.
/.open-next/
/.wrangler/

# prisma generated client
/src/generated/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files — real secrets never enter the repo, the template does
.env*
!.env.example

# Hand-off files for a connection string or a key, used for the length of one
# command and deleted afterwards. Ignored so that a production URL parked here
# for five minutes cannot be committed by an absent-minded `git add -A`.
prod-url.txt
*.secret.txt

# One-off reproduction scripts written at the repo root so they can import the
# project's dependencies. They are diagnostic scaffolding, not tooling — the
# ones worth keeping become scripts/verify-*.mjs. One rode into a commit on a
# `git add -A` before this line existed.
/repro*.mjs

# The integration runner's machine-readable report. Regenerated every run.
.vitest-integration.json

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# The rate-confirmation corpus (Phase 5 §2), AND its truth sheets.
#
# Real broker documents from live freight: names, addresses, phone numbers,
# rates and reference numbers belonging to the carrier's actual customers.
# They stay on the machine and in the dev bucket, and never in git — the
# repository is not the place for another company's paperwork.
#
# THE TRUTH SHEETS ARE IGNORED TOO, and the first draft of this rule was wrong
# to say otherwise. A sheet reading `brokerName: Cascade Freight Partners,
# rate: $2,450` is the same customer data as the PDF it came from, in a form
# that is easier to read. That it is derived rather than scanned changes
# nothing about whose information it is.
#
# The cost: §5's accuracy run is not reproducible from a clean checkout — the
# instrument lives beside the corpus on the owner's machine, and a number
# printed from it has to be pasted rather than re-derived by a reviewer. That
# is the trade this makes, and it is the owner's to reverse.
/corpus/

# THE AMAZON CORPUS — real booking emails and their Excel attachments.
#
# Same ruling as ./corpus and for the same reason (PHASE-5-BRIEF.md §7 flag 9):
# these carry customer names, facility addresses, appointment times, reference
# numbers and rates. The DERIVED truth sheets carry the same information in a
# form that is easier to read than the spreadsheet, not less sensitive than it,
# so they are ignored too — the cost being that an accuracy table has to be
# pasted from a run on the owner's machine rather than re-derived from a clean
# checkout.
/corpus-amazon/

# Proof that this working copy passed the integration suite, recently, here.
# NOT committed, and that is the point: a receipt cannot travel between
# machines, cannot be committed by accident, and cannot be reviewed into
# existence. It is evidence about one working copy at one moment, and
# `deploy:prod` checks it against HEAD, the tree, the endpoint and the clock.
/.integration-receipt.json

# Written by tests/failure-reporter.ts as each failure happens, so a run that
# dies mid-render still says which tests failed. Evidence, not source.
.integration-failures.log
.integration-failures.log.count

# Raw model responses, dumped by `doc-read.mjs`, `doc-accuracy.mjs` and
# `doc-variance.mjs` beside whatever they measured.
#
# SAME RULING AS ./corpus, AND FOR A SHARPER REASON: these are verbatim
# readings of real customer documents — a driver's licence number, a carrier's
# policy number, an owner-operator's VINs — held only so a surprising summary
# can be checked against what actually came back. They are evidence about one
# run on one machine, they belong beside the document they read, and neither
# git nor prettier has any business touching them.
/.extractions/
